{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790259167,"nonce":"xmCSm1+AKn3E8f6//+20Z2NBr+4=","title":"Python: a local rebound to another type keeps the join of both","message":"Locals are typed flow-insensitively: every assignment to a name joins\ninto one type. `data = array('B', data)` in pyaes CBCMode.encrypt and\ndecrypt joins the parameter (Object) with the array, so `data` stays\nObject and every `data[offset:offset+16]` and store goes through the\ndynamic layer. The same `x = f(x)` shape is in tokenize, pyflate and\nhexiom2.\n\nFix: a pre-inference rewrite (crates/zyntax_python/src/rebind.rs) that\ngives a straight-line rebinding a fresh name (x, x$1, ...) when it is\nthe only binding every later occurrence can see. A rebinding in a loop,\none a merge can read past, and a name read by a later-running body\n(def, lambda, class, every part of a generator expression but its\noutermost iterable) keep one name.\n\nVerify: ZYNTAX_TRACE_TYPES=1 on crypto_pyaes shows\n`locals pyaes$CBCMode$encrypt { ..., data$1: List(Array(UB)), ... }`.","files":null}]}